---
// ProjectFirma 2.0 base layout — wires the hub tokens + the ProjectFirma 2.0 theme,
// loads the brand's fonts, and sets data-theme="firma2" so every
// inherited esa-* component re-skins to ProjectFirma 2.0.
import '@esa/tokens/tokens.css';
import '@esa/tokens/component-tokens.css';
import '@esa/tokens/typography.css';
import '@esa/tokens/layouts.css';
// The page-level focus ring. Light DOM only — the esa-* web components paint
// their own from the same tokens. Without this a spoke's own <a>/<button> fall
// back to the browser default, which differs per engine and is user-editable.
import '@esa/tokens/focus.css';
import '@esa/tokens/a11y.css';
import '../styles/theme-firma2.css';
import '../styles/tenant-brand.css';
import '../styles/interface-prefs.css';
// Runtime handoff inspector — self-gating: dormant unless the current route
// has a bundle at public/handoff/<slug>/ (generated via `npx handoff`).
import HandoffInspector from '@esa/handoff/HandoffInspector.astro';
// The walkthrough bar for guided flows (?flow=…) — prototype chrome, inert
// until a flow is started in this tab. Mounted here so it follows the reader
// onto pages with and without the app shell.
import Firma2FlowGuide from '../components/shared/firma2-flow-guide.astro';
import Firma2Lightbox from '../components/shared/firma2-lightbox.astro';

// ACCESSIBILITY ASSURANCE (optional). If this project has a conformance obligation
// — Section 508, EN 301 549, a procurement clause — add data-a11y-assurance="wcag-aa" to
// the <html> tag below. It composes with data-theme; the brand does not change:
//
//   <html lang="en" data-theme="firma2" data-a11y-assurance="wcag-aa">
//
// It ships inside @esa/tokens and is inert until set, so there is nothing to import.
//
// YOUR THEME CARRIES ITS OWN, and that is the half that actually moves your brand. A
// theme written by `npm run theme:make` emits its own [data-a11y-assurance="wcag-aa"] block
// alongside the brand block, scoped so it outranks both. Grep theme-firma2.css
// for `data-a11y-assurance` to see exactly what the attribute changes for this project — it
// is usually short, because a generated theme is already AA in both schemes.
//
// IT CHANGES COLOUR AND NOTHING ELSE. Solid fills that carry text move to a
// contrast-safe step. Your components do not change: not their size, not their type,
// not one pixel of layout — verified across all 91 pages of the hub's own site. So
// size="xs" still renders at xs, and your size ramp keeps all four distinct steps.
//
// Anything that WOULD require a component to change is reported instead, and names
// the compliant option to use:
//
//   npm run a11y:sizes
//
// THE HUB'S HALF CANNOT REACH YOUR BRAND, and do not expect it to. `[data-theme]` and
// a bare `[data-a11y-assurance]` have equal specificity and theme-firma2.css loads
// later, so every role your theme re-points wins over the hub's profile — and the hub's
// values point at ITS brand ramp (grass), which would be wrong here anyway. That is why
// the block in your own theme file exists. Verify rather than assume, in both schemes:
//
//   node ../ecology/scripts/check-themes.mjs
//
// That one command grades every theme-*.css in src/styles across both schemes, with and
// without each profile — the four runs per theme that check-contrast.mjs takes one at a
// time. It exits 1 on any failure, so it belongs in this project's own `npm test`.
//
// DO NOT HAND-DECLARE THESE NAMES in a block of your own. Regenerating the theme is the
// channel; a hand-written override at the same specificity loses to whichever loads last.
//
// Setting the attribute is a statement about DEFAULTS, not a conformance certificate.
// See packages/tokens/src/assurance.css for what a profile cannot fix.

interface Props {
  title?: string;
}
const { title = 'ProjectFirma 2.0' } = Astro.props;
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="firma2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover, interactive-widget=resizes-content" />
    {/* The browser chrome takes the colour at the very top of the page: the app
        frame (--color-background-elevation-sunken = olive-3 light, #0d0e0c dark).
        One tag per scheme for a first paint that follows the OS; applyScheme() in
        src/lib/scheme.ts rewrites both when data-scheme is toggled by hand. */}
    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" content="#eff1ef" />
    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" content="#0d0e0c" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    {/* THE COLOUR SCHEME, APPLIED BEFORE FIRST PAINT.
        `is:inline` and blocking on purpose: a bundled module script is deferred,
        so a dark-scheme reader would watch the page render light and then flip
        on every single navigation. This is the one script in the app that has to
        run in <head>, which is also why it cannot import src/lib/scheme.ts —
        an inline script has no module graph. The four lines below are a
        DELIBERATE DUPLICATE of readScheme() + resolveScheme() there; the note at
        the top of that file says the same thing from the other side. Change one,
        change the other.

        Wrapped in try/catch because localStorage THROWS (rather than returning
        null) in a storage-partitioned context, and a throw here — in <head>,
        before anything — would take the whole page down to style an attribute. */}
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        var pref;
        try { pref = localStorage.getItem('firma2-scheme'); } catch (e) { pref = null; }
        if (pref !== 'light' && pref !== 'dark' && pref !== 'system') pref = 'system';
        var dark = pref === 'dark'
          || (pref === 'system' && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
        document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-scheme', dark ? 'dark' : 'light');
        // Explicit light/dark outranks the OS: pin both theme-color tags to it.
        if (pref !== 'system') {
          var tc = dark ? '#0d0e0c' : '#eff1ef', m = document.querySelectorAll('meta[name="theme-color"]');
          for (var j = 0; j < m.length; j++) m[j].setAttribute('content', tc);
        }
        document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-scheme-preference', pref);
      })();
    </script>
    {/* INTERFACE PREFERENCES — underline links, pointer cursors — before first
        paint, for the same reason as the scheme. A DELIBERATE DUPLICATE of
        readPref() + applyPref() in src/lib/interface-prefs.ts; change one,
        change the other. Only the non-default state writes an attribute. */}
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        var u, p;
        try {
          u = localStorage.getItem('firma2-underline-links');
          p = localStorage.getItem('firma2-pointer-cursors');
        } catch (e) { return; }
        if (u === 'on') document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-underline-links', '');
        if (p === 'off') document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-pointer-cursors', 'off');
      })();
    </script>
    {/* THE TENANT'S BRAND, applied before first paint for the same reason. A
        DELIBERATE DUPLICATE of brandVars() + onBrand() in src/lib/tenant-brand.ts
        — change the percentages there, change them here. */}
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        try {
          var b = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('firma2:tenant-brand:v1') || 'null');
          if (!b || !/^#[0-9a-f]{6}$/i.test(b.primary)) return;
          var h = b.primary, s = document.documentElement.style, i;
          var T = [4, 8, 14, 22, 32, 46, 62, 80], S = [88, 62, 38], DT = [3, 6, 14, 22, 30, 38, 48, 60], DL = [88, 65, 30];
          for (i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-' + (i + 1), 'color-mix(in oklab, ' + h + ' ' + T[i] + '%, white)');
            s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-dark-' + (i + 1), 'color-mix(in oklab, ' + h + ' ' + DT[i] + '%, #111210)');
          }
          for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-' + (i + 10), 'color-mix(in oklab, ' + h + ' ' + S[i] + '%, black)');
            s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-dark-' + (i + 10), 'color-mix(in oklab, ' + h + ' ' + DL[i] + '%, white)');
          }
          s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-9', h);
          s.setProperty('--tenant-brand-dark-9', h);
          var lum = function (x) {
            var v = parseInt(x.slice(1), 16), c = [(v >> 16) & 255, (v >> 8) & 255, v & 255].map(function (n) {
              n /= 255; return n <= 0.03928 ? n / 12.92 : Math.pow((n + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4);
            });
            return 0.2126 * c[0] + 0.7152 * c[1] + 0.0722 * c[2];
          };
          var L = lum(h), white = 1.05 / (L + 0.05), dark = (L + 0.05) / (lum('#1b1d1a') + 0.05);
          s.setProperty('--tenant-on-brand', white >= dark ? '#ffffff' : '#1b1d1a');
          document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-tenant-brand', '');
        } catch (e) { /* storage blocked or corrupt — the house brand stands */ }
      })();
    </script>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <!-- TODO(spoke-init): add the brand's Google Fonts <link> tags -->
    <title>{title}</title>
    <style is:global>
      /* CLOSED OVERLAYS STAY CLOSED WHILE THEIR SCRIPT LOADS. The page paints
         before the deferred module scripts define the legos, and an undefined
         dialog has no shadow root to keep its content shut — the Shortcuts
         dialog's rows were painted over the page on every navigation. These
         are all hidden at rest, so hiding them until :defined costs nothing. */
      :is(esa-dialog, esa-side-dialog, esa-confirm-dialog, esa-command-palette, esa-snackbar-container):not(:defined) {
        display: none;
      }
      *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
      /* THE SEARCH OVERLAY HAS TO LIFT WITHOUT A SCRIM. This theme pins the
         overlay backdrop to transparent, so esa-entity-search's shadow is its
         whole elevation — and the hub's --elevation-6 is one diffuse layer at
         20% black in BOTH schemes: soft-edged in light, invisible in dark,
         where the panel read as one more card. Tokens re-pinned on the host
         only (they inherit into its shadow root), so no other elevation-6
         surface moves. Dark also lifts the highlighted row: `sunken` there is
         neutral-0, darker than the panel, so the active result read as a hole.
         bcn-lego-checked: token re-pin on an esa- lego's host, no UI primitive;
         the lego's own border and fills are shadow-DOM hub code. Here, not in
         theme-firma2.css, because that file is generated. */
      esa-entity-search {
        --elevation-6:
          0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06),
          0 4px 12px -2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08),
          0 24px 64px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.24);
      }
      html[data-scheme="dark"] esa-entity-search {
        --elevation-6:
          0 2px 6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5),
          0 28px 72px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.8);
        --color-background-elevation-sunken: var(--firma2-neutral-5);
      }
      /* Its entrance, on the same host. The lego reads --animation-enter but the
         token is defined nowhere, so it ran on a generic ease-out and ignored
         the house curve this theme pins; and it has no reduced-motion path at
         all, which is the part that was actually missing. */
      esa-entity-search { --animation-enter: 140ms var(--easing-enter, ease-out); }
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        esa-entity-search { --animation-enter: 0s; }
      }
      /* The BROWSER'S half of the scheme — scrollbars, form-control internals,
         the canvas behind an over-scroll, and the default of anything the token
         layer does not paint. `data-scheme` alone moves our tokens and leaves
         all of that light, which reads as a bright frame around a dark page.
         Declared here rather than in theme-firma2.css because that file is
         GENERATED by `npm run theme:make` and a hand-edit would be overwritten;
         this is also not a brand fact — every spoke's dark scheme wants it. */
      html { color-scheme: light; }
      html[data-scheme="dark"] { color-scheme: dark; }
      /* NATIVE-FEEL BASELINE (mobile-native skill; .scratch/mobile-foundation/03).
         bcn-lego-checked: platform-layer html/media rules and a token re-pin, no
         UI primitive; esa-button renders in light DOM so the control rule reaches
         it, the Lit legos' shadow-root buttons are a hub gap.
         The pane, not the document, is the scroller, so the root can refuse the
         rubber band and pull-to-refresh. */
      html {
        -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
        -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
        text-size-adjust: 100%;
        overscroll-behavior: none;
      }
      body { overscroll-behavior: none; }
      /* Controls, never content: no double-tap-zoom delay, no long-press text
         selection or callout on a button label. */
      button, a, [role="button"] {
        touch-action: manipulation;
        user-select: none;
        -webkit-user-select: none;
        -webkit-touch-callout: none;
      }
      /* iOS zooms the page into any focused field under 16px. The md field value
         is body-md (textarea) / microcopy-md-subtle (text-field, select), both
         --font-size-200 (12.3px at 390). Re-point those two composites, which
         inherit into the legos' shadow roots, instead of hard-coding a size.
         html[data-theme] outranks the hub's :root declaration. */
      @media (pointer: coarse) {
        html[data-theme] {
          --typography-body-md-font-size: max(1rem, var(--font-size-200));
          --typography-microcopy-md-subtle-font-size: max(1rem, var(--font-size-200));
        }
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        font-family: var(--typography-font-family-sans, system-ui, sans-serif);
        font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-regular, 350);
        color: var(--color-content-default, #3d3d3d);
        /* The CANVAS role, not the raised one. The body is the page ground —
           the bottom of the elevation ramp that the bar and cards step off, so
           it cannot read the same token they do. This said `-elevation-raised`
           until 2026-08-20, when that role moved to neutral-2 to give the top
           bar its step; leaving it here would have dragged every non-app page
           (the landing index and the whole design-system browser, which render
           through BaseLayout without esa-app-shell painting a canvas over them)
           down onto the bar's surface. Light value is unchanged either way. */
        background: var(--color-background-default, #fff);
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
      }
      a { color: var(--color-content-link, #43608a); text-decoration: none; }
      img { display: block; max-width: 100%; }
      button { font-family: inherit; cursor: pointer; background: none; border: 0; }

      /* SECONDARY BUTTONS ARE OUTLINE BUTTONS in this spoke (user, 2026-11-02) —
         a deliberate DEVIATION from Ecology, whose secondary is a brand step-3
         wash with a gray border. Here it rests transparent with brand text and
         a brand border; the step-3 wash shows only on hover/press. Covers
         appearance="outline" too, whose hub border is the near-invisible step 3.
         The border is step 9 (--color-background-brand, the primary fill) —
         the step the hub's own outline appearance draws, and the first brand
         step that clears 3:1 against the page (steps 6-8 do not).
         Spoke-only by choice; the hub change was declined to keep other spokes
         as they are. html[data-theme] (0,1,1) outranks the lego's scoped rules.
         bcn-lego-checked: re-skins the esa-button secondary variant, no new UI
         primitive; esa-button is light DOM so a global rule reaches it. */
      html[data-theme] .esa-button--variant-secondary:is(.esa-button--appearance-fill, .esa-button--appearance-outline) > .esa-button__native {
        background: transparent;
        color: var(--color-content-brand);
        border-color: var(--color-background-brand, #46a758);
      }
      html[data-theme] .esa-button--variant-secondary:is(.esa-button--appearance-fill, .esa-button--appearance-outline) > .esa-button__native:hover:not(:disabled) {
        background: var(--color-background-brand-muted);
      }
      html[data-theme] .esa-button--variant-secondary.esa-button--active:is(.esa-button--appearance-fill, .esa-button--appearance-outline) > .esa-button__native {
        background: var(--color-background-brand-muted-hover);
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <slot />
    <Firma2FlowGuide />
    <Firma2Lightbox />
    <HandoffInspector />
    <script>
      // A "System" reader tracks the OS for as long as the tab is open — the
      // inline script above only reads the OS once, at load. Registered from the
      // BASE layout so it covers every page, not only the screen that owns the
      // control: the setting is app-wide, so its live behaviour has to be too.
      import { watchSystemScheme } from '../lib/scheme';
      import { syncShadowCursors } from '../lib/interface-prefs';
      watchSystemScheme();
      // "Use pointer cursors" off has to reach inside the legos' shadow roots,
      // which the inline script above cannot. No-op while the setting is on.
      syncShadowCursors();
    </script>
    <script>
    // The tenant's vocabulary, applied to every page's marked labels and the
    // nav. See src/lib/vocabulary.ts.
    import { bootVocabulary } from '../lib/vocabulary';
    bootVocabulary();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
